<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      Release-only manifest: strips the network permissions.

      NLR Authenticator never opens a socket. It generates codes from a stored
      secret and the device clock, and it has no backend to talk to.

      Two permissions arrive here anyway, merged in from a dependency:

        INTERNET and ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE come from the ML Kit barcode library
        that mobile_scanner uses. ML Kit declares them for optional telemetry
        and for the *unbundled* model, which is downloaded through Play
        Services. This project uses the BUNDLED model
        (com.google.mlkit:barcode-scanning) - the detector ships inside the APK
        and scans with no network at all - so neither permission is needed for
        anything the app actually does.

      Removing them turns "this app does not use the network" from a promise in
      a settings screen into something the operating system enforces. A user can
      verify it themselves in the app's permission list.

      This file applies to RELEASE builds only. Debug builds keep INTERNET,
      because the Flutter tool needs it for hot reload and the debugger - see
      src/debug/AndroidManifest.xml. That is the correct split: the guarantee
      matters for the build people install, and hot reload matters for the build
      developers run.

      If you ever switch mobile_scanner to the unbundled model
      (dev.steenbakker.mobile_scanner.useUnbundled=true), delete this file -
      scanning would then genuinely need the network to fetch its model.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" tools:node="remove" />

</manifest>
